## Closed typed action fields
For every new or modified control action, `effect_kind` is a closed enum. Use
exactly one of these values; do not invent synonyms such as
`internal_state_update`, `message`, or `operation`:

- `model_output`: text or structured output returned to the caller.
- `tool_call`: a structured operation invocation emitted by the agent.
- `state_change`: a session or persistent state update.
- `agent_message`: an internal message to another responsibility or controller.
- `environment_action`: an external side effect beyond output, invocation, or
  state mutation.

The target and effect must agree. A control action targeting a `responsibility`
is an internal controller message and MUST use `effect_kind: "agent_message"`.
Do not use `state_change` for that target merely because the action updates a
process model. Preserve the exact typed value shown for an existing action.

`temporality` is also closed: use exactly `instantaneous`, `discrete`,
`continuous`, `bounded_duration`, or `unknown`; do not infer it from wording.

## Context fields
The values, evidence refs, operation, process-model refs, source, source kind,
and authority for update shown under existing elements are typed fields. Copy
them into any responsibility you restate. For new elements:
- process model parts carry `values` (distinct decision-relevant values) and
  `evidence_refs` (exact discovered-evidence refs, or `[]`);
- control actions carry `operation` (exact observed operation name, or `null`)
  and `process_model_refs` (PM IDs of the same responsibility that decide
  whether the action is safe);
- feedback channels carry `source_kind`: `operation_result`,
  `session_context`, `user_message`, `conversation_history`,
  `retrieved_content`, or `other`.

A feedback channel's `source` and a process model part's `feedback_source` are
element references, not kinds: `{"type": ..., "id": ...}`, where `type` is
exactly `responsibility` (with a `RESP-*` ID) or `controlled_process` (with a
`CP-*` ID) and names the element the information comes from. A source line
shown as `controlled_process CP-1` is the reference
`{"type": "controlled_process", "id": "CP-1"}`. Write the kind of information
only in `source_kind`; a source kind is never a reference `type`.

A feedback channel may also carry `authority_for_update`, with a `status` and a
`basis`: whether the channel is entitled to establish the value of the PM it
`updates`, as opposed to only reporting it. `status` is `authoritative` (the
evidence makes the channel a source that may set the value), `non_authoritative`
(the evidence shows it can report the value but not establish it),
`conditional` (it may establish the value only within a scope or under
conditions the evidence states), or `unknown` (the evidence bears on the
question but leaves it open). Assess the particular value: an untrusted user
message may be authoritative for that user's own request, requested date,
answers, or operation arguments, while remaining non-authoritative for a value
the system must establish independently, such as eligibility, record ownership,
available capacity, or another person's approval.

`basis` states the scope and any conditions in domain words, without
control-structure IDs, and cites evidence the request actually supplies: say
`Use case: ...` and identify the relevant wording, or include the exact `ref` of
a discovered target-evidence record. A bare ref such as
`state:records.owner_id`, `user input is unverified`, and `user-provided text`
are not sufficient bases. It is required unless
`status` is `unknown`. Assess it for a new channel, and for a restated channel
shown without one, where the supplied evidence bears on it; omit it otherwise.
Omitting it leaves a new channel, or a restated channel shown without one,
`unknown`; a restated channel that still updates the PM shown with it keeps the
assessment shown. An assessment covers only the PM shown with it: when a
restated channel updates a different PM, the assessment shown is not kept, so
assess it again or omit it to leave it `unknown`. An assessment dropped because
its basis fails these requirements leaves the channel `unknown`, not with the
assessment shown. Do not derive authority from
`source_kind`: a trusted source kind does not make a channel authoritative, and
an untrusted one does not by itself make it non-authoritative.

